@@ -277,0 +281,30 @@ The agent has the same filesystem access as your user account. Use `hermes tools
+#### Optional macOS local write confinement
+
+On macOS, the local backend can place the child processes created by `terminal`
+(foreground, background pipe, and PTY forms) and `execute_code` inside the same
+Seatbelt write boundary:
+
+```yaml
+terminal:
+  backend: local
+  local_confinement:
+    provider: macos-seatbelt
+    writable_roots:
+      - /absolute/path/to/workspace
+```
+
+Every writable root must already exist, resolve to an absolute canonical directory,
+and may not be `/`. Hermes also grants the exact private runtime directory needed for
+temporary process and kernel files. Child processes may read host files, use IP
+networking, listen on sockets, and start descendants, but outbound Unix-domain socket
+connections and filesystem writes outside those roots are denied; descendants inherit
+the boundary. Pair this with `HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT` when the
+declarative `write_file` and `patch` tools must follow the same workspace policy.
+
+The provider is opt-in and macOS-only. When configured, an unknown provider, missing
+`/usr/bin/sandbox-exec`, invalid root, or policy setup failure aborts the child spawn;
+Hermes does not retry it without confinement. Changing the policy starts a fresh
+`execute_code` session kernel. Apple has deprecated `sandbox-exec`, so deployments that
+need a long-term cross-platform security boundary should continue to use an isolated
+backend such as Docker, Modal, Daytona, or Vercel Sandbox.
+
