+### macOS local child write boundary
+
+The optional `terminal.local_confinement.provider: macos-seatbelt` setting addresses
+the shell/interpreter bypass above for local `terminal` and `execute_code` children.
+It applies at all four local child-spawn shapes—foreground shell, background pipe,
+background PTY, and persistent Python kernel—and descendants inherit the policy.
+Writes are limited to canonical `writable_roots` plus Hermes's exact private runtime
+directory. The active provider is included in tool/process results as
+`execution_confinement` so callers can distinguish enforced from ordinary local runs.
+
+This is a **direct-filesystem-write boundary**, not complete isolation: host reads, IP
+network access, inbound sockets, process creation, IPC, and signals remain available
+(outbound Unix-domain sockets are denied except for macOS DNS resolution). A process
+can therefore read or exfiltrate material visible to the Hermes OS account, or ask a
+reachable HTTP service to mutate state. It is macOS-only, relies on the
+deprecated `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec` interface, and is disabled by default. Use a
+container or remote sandbox when reads, network, process visibility, portability, or
+stronger long-term isolation are part of the threat model. Configuration and failure
+semantics are documented under [Optional macOS local write confinement](./configuration.md#optional-macos-local-write-confinement).
+
